 Launch page for Meridian, a timeline-based motion editor for the web.
 
-Built with Next.js, React, and Tailwind CSS.
+The page is built as one 24-second composition: **scroll is the playhead**.
+A raw-WebGL2 particle field (one instanced draw call, positions a pure
+function of time — perfectly scrubbable), a persistent timeline HUD with
+live SMPTE timecode, and two working instruments (a master easing-curve
+editor and a spring lab) whose values re-choreograph the field live and
+ship verbatim in the final export block.
+
+Built with Next.js, React, and Tailwind CSS. No animation or 3D libraries.
 
 ## Development
 
@@ -10,3 +17,23 @@ Built with Next.js, React, and Tailwind CSS.
 npm install
 npm run dev
 ```
+
+## Structure
+
+- `lib/engine.js` — the playhead engine: scroll→time, playback (K),
+  visibility/intersection/idle pausing, act measurement
+- `lib/field.js` — WebGL2 renderer: five scenes blended by playhead,
+  master-ease + spring + pointer uniforms, capability tiers
+- `lib/motion.js` — bezier solver, spring integrator, timecode
+- `components/Experience.jsx` — the five acts, choreography, export, form
+- `components/{Hud,BezierEditor,SpringLab}.jsx` — the instruments
+
+## Accessibility & performance notes
+
+- Reduced motion (OS setting or the HUD toggle) switches to a static
+  storyboard layout: natural flow, single deterministic field frames,
+  timing shown as dot-spacing and step-response plots instead of movement.
+- Rendering pauses when the tab is hidden, the canvas leaves the viewport,
+  or after 90 s without input; an adaptive governor sheds particle density
+  if frames run long. Mobile gets a no-pinning layout, lower density, and
+  a 1.5 DPR cap.
